===============
Day 2: Commands
===============
1. make clean

---Make changes as needed before compiling the kernel 
	- makes changes to the .config file before compiling the code
	- this gives you the chance to make changes fix error and stuff then compile boot and test 
		- .config = blueprint for what the kernel should contain
			- what drivers to include
			- what features to enable
			- what modules to build
			- what architecture settings to use
2. make menuconfig
	- Search for 'Kernel hacking'
		- enable debug options
		- enable extra logging
	- Change Hostname/Version
		- search localversion
			- set something like 'JoshLab'
2b. make defconfig

---Tie all the files togther to create the linux kerel 3-3b
	-The kenel starts as thousands of seperate files
	-The below commands compiles them/links them together to create the 'KERNEL IMAGE'
3. make = one task at a time
3b. make -j$(nproc) = build using all available resources
	-j - run multiple builds simultaneouslly
	-nproc - returns the number of cpu cores/threads
		- so command: make -j8 (if you had 8 cores)

---Location of the Kernel Image File
4. arch/x86/boot/bzImage
	- name is typically vmlinux

---Install vs Boot
5. sudo make modules_install # copies modules into the /lib/modules/<kernal-version>/ <- directory
5b. sudo make install # installs kernel image into /boot
	-Now the machine knows the kernel exists
	-This means that when the machine reboots you can select that kernel

